{"causes": ["The established Roman cursus honorum provided a traditional path for ambitious men like Agrippa to ascend through magistracies, culminating in the consulship.", "His father, Quintus Haterius, a distinguished orator and close associate of Augustus, provided Decimus with significant social capital and political connections within the Roman elite.", "The consolidation of imperial power under Tiberius meant that political advancement and survival increasingly depended on the emperor's favor and avoiding his suspicion."], "effects": ["Agrippa's consulship in 22 CE contributed to the ongoing functioning of the Roman administrative system, even as imperial authority overshadowed republican institutions.", "His death in 32 CE, during Tiberius's purges, exemplified the increasing vulnerability of the senatorial class to imperial suspicion and the arbitrary application of treason laws.", "The fate of Agrippa and others during the purges reinforced the absolute nature of the emperor's power, discouraging open dissent and consolidating Tiberius's control over the Roman elite."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "quintus-haterius", "sourceName": "Quintus Haterius", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "decimus-haterius-agrippa", "targetName": "Decimus Haterius Agrippa", "context": "Quintus Haterius's prominence as an orator and senator provided Decimus Haterius Agrippa with significant familial advantage in his political career."}, {"sourceSlug": "decimus-haterius-agrippa", "sourceName": "Decimus Haterius Agrippa", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "roman-senate", "targetName": "Roman Senate", "context": "Decimus Haterius Agrippa served as a prominent member of the Roman Senate, holding various magistracies including the consulship in 22 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "tiberius", "sourceName": "Tiberius", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "decimus-haterius-agrippa", "targetName": "Decimus Haterius Agrippa", "context": "Emperor Tiberius heavily influenced Decimus Haterius Agrippa's political career and ultimately ordered his execution in 32 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "decimus-haterius-agrippa", "sourceName": "Decimus Haterius Agrippa", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "rome", "targetName": "Rome", "context": "Decimus Haterius Agrippa's entire political career and life unfolded within the city of Rome, the heart of the Roman Empire."}, {"sourceSlug": "sejanus", "sourceName": "Sejanus", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "decimus-haterius-agrippa", "targetName": "Decimus Haterius Agrippa", "context": "The downfall and subsequent purges initiated by Tiberius after Sejanus's conspiracy in 31 CE directly led to Decimus Haterius Agrippa's execution in 32 CE."}], "places": [{"name": "Rome, Roman Empire", "role": "Center of political career"}, {"name": "Capri, Roman Empire", "role": "Tiberius's residence during purges"}, {"name": "Italy, Roman Empire", "role": "Homeland"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Decimus Haterius Agrippa", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "decimus-haterius-agrippa"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q763627", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decimus_Haterius_Agrippa"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 5, "significanceNarrative": "Decimus Haterius Agrippa's career and tragic demise exemplify the shifting power dynamics between the Roman Senate and the emperor during the early Principate, demonstrating the increasing fragility of senatorial status under a suspicious ruler like Tiberius. His death during the purges of 32 CE highlights the brutal consolidation of imperial power.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-19T15:28:29Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32268503159", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Decimus Haterius Agrippa was a Roman plebeian tribune, praetor and consul. He was the son of the orator and senator Quintus Haterius and his wife Vipsania.", "newValue": "Decimus Haterius Agrippa, a member of the prominent Haterii family, navigated the complex political landscape of the early Roman Principate, serving as plebeian tribune, praetor, and ultimately consul in 22 CE. Born to the renowned orator and senator Quintus Haterius and Vipsania, his career trajectory illustrates the continued importance of familial influence and senatorial tradition even as imperial power consolidated under Tiberius. Agrippa's elevation to the consulship, the highest magistracy in the Roman Republic and early Empire, marked a significant achievement for his family, a relatively new senatorial house that had risen to prominence under Augustus.

His political career was characterized by adherence to the established senatorial cursus honorum. After holding the tribuneship of the plebs, Agrippa served as praetor in 21 CE. A notable event during his praetorship involved a senatorial debate concerning the appropriate sum to be paid to the governor of Asia for his province, highlighting the Senate's continued, albeit constrained, role in administrative affairs. His consulship in 22 CE placed him at the heart of Roman governance during a period of increasing tension between the Emperor Tiberius and the senatorial class, a tension exacerbated by the growing influence of the Praetorian Prefect Sejanus.

Agrippa's life ended tragically in 32 CE, caught in the sweeping purges initiated by Tiberius following the downfall of Sejanus. Though not directly implicated in Sejanus's conspiracy, Agrippa was among those accused of various offenses, including treason, a common charge used to eliminate political rivals or perceived threats to the emperor's authority. His execution, alongside other distinguished Romans, underscored the precariousness of senatorial life under a suspicious emperor and the brutal efficiency with which political opposition could be suppressed, marking a dark chapter in the history of the Julio-Claudian dynasty."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-19T15:28:29Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32268503159", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "19 BCE"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "19 BCE"}], "newValue": ["The established Roman cursus honorum provided a traditional path for ambitious men like Agrippa to ascend through magistracies, culminating in the consulship.", "His father, Quintus Haterius, a distinguished orator and close associate of Augustus, provided Decimus with significant social capital and political connections within the Roman elite.", "The consolidation of imperial power under Tiberius meant that political advancement and survival increasingly depended on the emperor's favor and avoiding his suspicion."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-19T15:28:29Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32268503159", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "32 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "32 CE"}], "newValue": ["Agrippa's consulship in 22 CE contribu